<section class="sec" style="padding-bottom:24px">
<div class="narrow">
<p class="lead">The Florida Keys are world-renowned for tarpon, and Key West has long been a destination for anglers who want a shot at one of the hardest-fighting fish in our waters. Known as the Silver King, tarpon are famous for their size, blistering runs and acrobatic jumps once hooked.</p>
<p>For us, one of the best parts of Key West tarpon fishing is being able to sight-cast to migrating fish in clear water. Get the right conditions and you can watch these fish move through the shallows, get a cast in front of them and see the whole thing unfold.</p>
<p>Tarpon are the largest of our flats fish and a signature species here in Key West. Our peak migration occurs during the spring and early summer, bringing serious tarpon anglers to the Lower Keys for one of the most exciting sight-fishing opportunities of the year.</p>
<p>Whether you’ve spent years chasing tarpon or this will be your first attempt at catching one, our full-time professional fishing guides can put you on the water with the right equipment, instruction and local knowledge.</p>
<div class="rule"></div>
</div>
</section>

<section class="sec">
<div class="wrap two">
{% img "wix/95e38a_f81d7e9b7bb54199865b2558fd5f9c50.png", "Tarpon Fishing with Key West Pro Guides", "(max-width: 1023px) 100vw, 600px", "photo" %}
<div>
<h2>Why Fish for Tarpon in Key West?</h2>
<p>There are plenty of fish that pull hard. Tarpon are different.</p>
<p>These are true brutes of the shallows. Their combination of size, speed and aerial displays has made them one of the most sought-after gamefish in the Florida Keys. Hook one and the fight can include long runs followed by a fish launching completely out of the water.</p>
<p>That’s a big part of what keeps anglers coming back.</p>
<p>But the fight is only half the experience. Sight fishing is what makes chasing tarpon around Key West so special. Instead of simply dropping a bait and waiting, there are days when we’re actively looking for fish moving through clear water. Your guide may spot them first and begin positioning the boat so you have an opportunity to make the cast.</p>
<p>Sometimes you’ll see silver sides. Sometimes it’s a dark back, a tail slipping below the surface or a fish rolling and giving away its position.</p>
<p>Then the game begins.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>

<section class="sec tint">
<div class="wrap two">
<div>
<h2>A Little About the Silver King</h2>
<p>Tarpon have earned their reputation as one of the world’s great gamefish. They are powerful fish capable of tremendous runs and spectacular leaps, and their hard mouths can make getting a solid hook-set part of the challenge.</p>
<p>They can also grow to extraordinary sizes. That combination of size, power and the opportunity to see the fish before making your cast is why springtime tarpon fishing has such a strong following.</p>
<p>These aren’t fish you simply overpower. Finding them, getting into position, making a good presentation and staying connected once a fish eats all matter.</p>
<p>That’s also why having an experienced fishing guide beside you makes a difference. Our captains spend their working lives on these waters. They’ll help you understand what they’re seeing, where to cast and what to do when a tarpon finally eats.</p>
</div>
{% img "wix/95e38a_5002fbaa8a2e4b4abc5806b79a2cef24.jpg", "Tarpon Fishing with Key West Pro Guides", "(max-width: 1023px) 100vw, 600px", "photo" %}
</div>
</section>

<section class="sec">
<div class="wrap two">
{% img "wix/95e38a_182da5b154cd4f14888b6ce5e5aa32ca.jpg", "Tarpon Fishing with Key West Pro Guides", "(max-width: 1023px) 100vw, 600px", "photo" %}
<div>
<h2>Key West Flats Fishing for Tarpon</h2>
<p>Key West’s shallow-water fishery gives anglers the opportunity to pursue tarpon from our 24-foot bay boats. Tarpon fishing can take us into the same clear, shallow-water environment that makes flats fishing throughout the Lower Keys so popular.</p>
<p>When conditions line up, the experience can be extremely visual.<br>Polarized sunglasses help tremendously. Tarpon can be difficult to spot even when they’re close to the boat, and your guide may see a fish well before you do. Listen to the directions, look where your captain points and be ready to make the cast.</p>
<p>There are also days when these fish can be found away from the crowd. One of the experiences Key West Pro Guides has long emphasized is the opportunity to sight-cast to migrating tarpon without another boat in sight.</p>
<p>That combination of open water, clear conditions and a big Silver King coming into casting range is hard to beat.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>

<section class="sec tint">
<div class="wrap two">
<div>
<h2>Key West Tarpon Migration & Best Time to Fish</h2>
<p>Tarpon are a signature Key West species, and peak tarpon migration occurs during spring and early summer.</p>
<p>That’s the primary window anglers think about when planning a dedicated tarpon trip to the Florida Keys. These migrating fish move through the Lower Keys as part of their seasonal pattern, creating some of our most anticipated fishing of the year.</p>
<p>Conditions still matter. This is fishing, and there are no guarantees about what any individual day will bring. Wind, water and fish movement can all affect where we fish and how we approach the day.</p>
<p>That’s where local experience comes into play.</p>
<p>Our guides aren’t working from a script or fishing the same spot regardless of conditions. They’re full-time, owner-operator professional guides with deep local knowledge, and the goal is to make the most of the opportunities the day gives us.</p>
</div>
