<div>
<h2>How We Fish for Tarpon</h2>
<p>There isn’t just one way to catch a tarpon.</p>
<p>The existing Key West Pro Guides approach includes fly fishing, live bait and plugs, with the exact presentation depending on the situation. Tarpon are aggressive, opportunistic feeders, but that doesn’t mean they’ll eat anything thrown in their direction. A good presentation matters.</p>
<p>The most exciting opportunities are often the ones where you can see the fish first.</p>
<p>Your captain may spot rolling or traveling fish and position the boat to give you a shot. From there, you’ll get instruction on where to cast and how to work your presentation.</p>
<p>This is fishing where paying attention matters.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>

<section class="sec tint">
<div class="wrap two">
<div>
<h2>Sight Fishing and Reading the Water</h2>
<p>Tarpon can be surprisingly difficult to see for such a large fish.</p>
<p>When conditions are right, rolling tarpon can reveal themselves at the surface. You may catch a flash of silver, see a black back or notice a tail disappearing beneath the water.</p>
<p>A good pair of polarized sunglasses makes a real difference.</p>
<p>Once your guide points out the fish, don’t worry if you don’t immediately see exactly what they’re seeing. Experienced guides spend countless hours reading the water. Listen to the direction of the cast and be ready.</p>
<p>When casting to moving or rolling fish, the idea is to get the presentation where the tarpon can find it without making the fish work unnecessarily hard for it.</p>
<p>That sounds simple. When a big tarpon is moving toward you and the moment finally arrives, it feels like anything but.</p>
</div>
{% img "wix/95e38a_2b21813181c5402a9f100193428570eb.jpg", "Tarpon Fishing with Key West Pro Guides", "(max-width: 1023px) 100vw, 600px", "photo" %}
</div>
</section>

<section class="sec">
<div class="wrap two">
{% img "wix/95e38a_54cfc23b31a8465c84b85cc06e4902c0.jpg", "Two anglers with a tarpon in shallow Key West water", "(max-width: 1023px) 100vw, 600px", "photo" %}
<div>
<h2>Fly Fishing for Key West Tarpon</h2>
<p>For many anglers, fly fishing for tarpon is one of the ultimate shallow-water challenges.</p>
<p>A well-presented streamer fly placed ahead of a moving tarpon gives the fly time to get into position. The existing Key West Pro Guides guidance is to lead fish rather than casting directly on top of them, then work the fly with controlled, shorter strips.</p>
<p>The visual part is what makes it memorable.</p>
<p>You see the fish. You make the cast. You work the fly. Then, if everything comes together, you see a tarpon react.</p>
<p>Fly fishing isn’t the only way we pursue these fish, and you don’t need to arrive as an expert. Instruction is included in the charter, and our trips are built for anglers across different skill levels.</p>
<p>Experienced fly anglers can come looking for a serious shot at the Silver King. First-timers can come ready to listen, learn and enjoy the process.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>

<section class="sec tint">
<div class="wrap two">
<div>
<h2>Live Bait and Plugs</h2>
<p>Tarpon also respond to live bait and artificial presentations.</p>
<p>The existing page specifically identifies mullet and pinfish among the live baits used for tarpon and also notes their willingness to take topwater plugs. Which approach makes sense depends on the fishing situation and the opportunities your guide is seeing.</p>
<p>With plugs, the same basic idea applies: make a good presentation in front of the fish and work it in a way that gives the tarpon an easy opportunity to eat.</p>
<p>Once a tarpon takes a fly or plug, getting a solid hook-set matters. Tarpon have exceptionally hard mouths. Your captain will walk you through what to do, so when the moment comes, listen and react.</p>
<p>That’s one of the reasons we include instruction on every trip.</p>
<p>You don’t need to show up knowing every detail about tarpon fishing. That’s our job.</p>
</div>
